# 7. Stage 5 — Note editor and interaction

Use the ColorNote-style View-first model for existing notes.

### Existing note opening

Tapping an existing note opens it in **View mode**, not directly in Edit mode.

View mode:

- Displays the note title and content.
- Shows a subtle `Viewing` status.
- Shows the modified timestamp.
- Text content is read-only.
- Checklist checkboxes are interactive.
- Checking or unchecking an item updates persistent state.
- Checked checklist items display strikethrough.
- Provide a clear Edit action.
- Tapping the title or using the agreed body double-tap gesture may enter Edit mode, but do not make a gesture the only way to edit.

### New note opening

New Text Notes and new Checklists open directly in **Edit mode**.

- Focus the appropriate input.
- Use keyboard/IME handling that does not obscure the editor.
- Ensure the user can immediately enter content.

### Editor appearance

Use:

- Saturated, contrast-safe color header.
- Back navigation.
- Inline title field.
- Color swatch button.
- Overflow menu.
- Subtle metadata/status row below the header.
- Soft pastel paper body.
- Native Compose text input.

The color swatch opens a compact 3×3 color picker using the exact unified nine-color palette.

### Ruled notebook paper

Include faint horizontal notebook rules behind the text-note editing surface.

Requirements:

- Rules align with the text line height.
- Use a line height around 28–32sp, adjusted to the actual text style.
- Lines remain subtle and do not reduce readability.
- Text scrolling and cursor placement must remain correct.
- Do not implement this with a browser/WebView editor.

Use native Compose drawing or another implementation compatible with the existing project.

### Text editing and autosave

- Use native Compose text input, such as BasicTextField where appropriate.
- Support title and body editing.
- Persist changes through the Notes repository.
- Use debounced autosave.
- Save reliably when leaving the editor or navigating away.
- Avoid stale asynchronous writes overwriting newer edits.
- Do not lose edits during quick navigation or repeated updates.
- Keep modified timestamps correct.
- Handle empty notes and unfinished new notes deliberately.
- Provide explicit Discard behavior if a new unsaved note is abandoned.
- Avoid creating duplicate notes during recomposition or repeated navigation.

### Undo / Redo

Include Undo and Redo in Stage 1.

Use a proper in-memory snapshot history suitable for Compose, not browser `document.execCommand`.

History must cover:

- Title.
- Text body.
- Note color.
- Checklist item text.
- Checklist item order.
- Checklist checked state.

Requirements:

- A title edit must be undoable.
- Color changes must be undoable.
- Checklist changes must be undoable.
- Undo and Redo must not accidentally overwrite newer database state.
- Save the resulting current state safely.
- Disable Undo/Redo when no corresponding history exists.
- Do not create a history entry for every meaningless recomposition.
- Keep the implementation bounded to the current editing session unless the existing architecture already supports durable history.

